#include <simul_efun.h>

/**
 * Rejects with ASYNC_ERR_TIMEOUT once the given delay elapses, unless it is
 * cancelled first. Raced against real work by with_deadline() so a promise
 * that never settles gives up instead of parking the frame for the life of
 * the driver.
 *
 * It throws rather than errors: work running long is a routine outcome, and
 * error() would write a traceback to /log/catch and notify every online
 * developer for it.
 *
 * The cancellation its own body receives is caught and swallowed, because
 * being cancelled is how this arm is retired when the work wins. Without
 * that, releasing the loser would only swap one rejection for another.
 *
 * @param {float} seconds - Seconds to wait before rejecting.
 * @returns {int} 0, and only on the cancelled path — the delay elapsing
 *  rejects instead. The value is never read; it is an int rather than void so
 *  the promise this hands back carries a payload.
 * @throws ASYNC_ERR_TIMEOUT once the delay has elapsed.
 */
private async int deadline_timer(float seconds) {
  mixed err = acatch(await call_out_walltime(seconds));

  if(err)
    return 0;

  throw(ASYNC_ERR_TIMEOUT);
}

/**
 * Awaits a promise under a ceiling, so work that never settles gives up
 * instead of parking the awaiting frame for the life of the driver.
 *
 * The ceiling is a race against a timer, which is the only way to bound a
 * wait on a promise you do not own — most of them cannot be cancelled at all
 * (a promise_create() promise, async I/O, a call_out promise), and a race
 * stops your waiting rather than the work. What the work goes on to do after
 * the ceiling elapses is the caller's problem to handle, and callers with a
 * transfer to tear down do exactly that.
 *
 * The losing arm is released on every path, not just the winning one:
 * promise_race() does not stop its losers, so work that settles quickly
 * would otherwise leave the timer parked for the rest of its delay, holding
 * a call_out and a suspension slot. The release is a defer() rather than
 * code after the await so it also runs when this body is itself cancelled or
 * the work rejects. Cancelling a timer that already won returns 0 and does
 * nothing.
 *
 * A non-promise is returned as-is, so wrapping something that turns out to
 * be synchronous costs nothing and arms no timer. A non-positive ceiling
 * means no ceiling, which only makes sense for something certain to settle.
 *
 * @param {mixed} p - The promise to await, or any plain value.
 * @param {float} [seconds] - The ceiling in seconds; non-positive waits
 *  indefinitely.
 * @returns {mixed} What p fulfilled with, or p itself if it was not a
 *  promise.
 * @throws ASYNC_ERR_TIMEOUT if the ceiling elapses first, and whatever p
 *  rejected with otherwise.
 * @example
 * mixed rows = await with_deadline(SOME_D->fetch(), 3.0);
 */
async varargs mixed with_deadline(mixed p, float seconds) {
  promise<int> timer;

  if(!promisep(p))
    return p;

  if(seconds <= 0.0)
    return await p;

  timer = deadline_timer(seconds);

  defer((: promise_cancel($(timer)) :));

  return await promise_race(({ timer, p }));
}
